{"text": "Professional Resume\n\nA\nBIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH\nName: Position:\nTristan Seth Kimbrell Assistant Professor of Biology\nnttion & ection Degree Ye ag | Retlofstudy\n\u2018Temple University 4D. 2011 law\nPhiadelpnia, PA\nUniversity of Florida Pho. 2007 Zoology\nGaines, FL\nKansas State University as. 1999 Biology\nManhattan, KS\n\nB, PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCES DATE\nSouthern University at New Orleans, New Orleans, LA\nAssistant Professor of Biology 08/11/2014 ~ Present\nEnvironmental Protection Agency ~ Philadelphia, PA Law Clerk 05/2010 -08/2010\n\nPennsylvania Superior Court, Philadelphia, PA. Law Clerk 05/2009 - 08/2009\n(University of Miami, Coral Gables, FL.\n\n(C. MAJOR RESEARCH INTERESTS\nMy primary research interest revolves around exploring the interaction between ecology and the law. |\n\u2018am curious to discover how federal environmental laws positively and negatively influence species and\necosystems. For many environmental laws, there has been relatively little scientific research into how\nthey impact the environments they regulate. As a secondary interest, ! am also curious to explore issues\nof environmental justice. Environmental degradation tends to disproportionately affect poor\n\u2018communities, and | want to study how altered ecological dynamics contribute to the burden felt by the\npoor. Finally, 1am interested in biological mechanisms that tend to promote or constrain species\nevolution in degraded habitats.\n\nD. PUBLICATIONS (Short list)\n1. _kimbrell,T. 2010. Moving species and non-moving reserves: conservation banking and the impact of\nglobal climate change. Fordham Environmental Law Review 22:119-157.\n2. Kimbrell, T. 2010. Canalization and adaption in a landscape of sources and sinks. Evolutionary\nEcology 24:891 909.\n3. Potts, M.D. and T. Kimbrell, 2010. Rules of thumb for the control of vector-borne diseases in a\nspatial environment. in: Spatial Ecology, eds. . Cantrell, C. Cosner, and S. Ruan. Chapman and\nHall/cRC\n4. Worman, C. and T. kimbrell, 2008, Getting to the hart of the matter: did antlers truly cause the\n\u2018extinction of the Irish elk? Oikos 117:1397-1405,",
